+++ b/src/data/posts/guterres-rohingya-still-await-accountability-icj-ruling-november-19.json
@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+{
+ "slug": "guterres-rohingya-still-await-accountability-icj-ruling-november-19",
+ "date": "2026-11-18T06:40:00",
+ "modified": "2026-11-18T06:40:00",
+ "title": "Rohingya still await accountability, Guterres says before ICJ ruling",
+ "excerpt": "The UN chief told the ASEAN Summit that states must protect people fleeing persecution, days before the world court rules in Gambia v. Myanmar.",
+ "categories": [
+  "news",
+  "policy"
+ ],
+ "tags": [
+  "humanitarian-rohingya-response",
+  "rohingya-response",
+  "human-rights",
+  "coxs-bazar"
+ ],
+ "image": null,
+ "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">UN Secretary-General Ant\u00f3nio Guterres has said the Rohingya \"still await accountability for grave violations\" nearly a decade after their forced mass displacement from Myanmar, UN News reported on 16 November. He was speaking at the ASEAN Summit in Manila.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Guterres said states have a duty to protect people fleeing violence until the conditions for a safe return are in place. \"Until these conditions are met, States have a duty to protect those fleeing violence and persecution,\" he said. UN News put the number of Rohingya living in the camps in Bangladesh at nearly 1.2 million.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">His remarks come three days before the International Court of Justice (ICJ) gives its judgment in The Gambia's genocide case against Myanmar, due on 19 November.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For agencies working in Cox's Bazar and Bhasan Char, the ruling and the UN's public line on accountability shape the case for keeping refugees protected and funded while return remains impossible. As Devlens <a href=\"/2026/11/14/rohingya-food-aid-funded-until-april-2027-rations-malnutrition/\">reported</a>, food rations are funded only until April 2027. A clear statement from the UN chief gives NGOs a reference point for advocacy with donors and ASEAN governments.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The ICJ judgment on 19 November, how Myanmar and ASEAN members respond, and whether donors link the ruling to new funding for the Rohingya response.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://news.un.org/en/story/2026/11/1168809\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">UN News: Rohingya still await accountability, Guterres tells ASEAN, 16 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/ldc-graduation-2029-roadmap-sovereign-bond-imf-deal-february.json b/src/data/posts/ldc-graduation-2029-roadmap-sovereign-bond-imf-deal-february.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..4f4299b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/data/posts/ldc-graduation-2029-roadmap-sovereign-bond-imf-deal-february.json
@@ -0,0 +1,18 @@
+{
+ "slug": "ldc-graduation-2029-roadmap-sovereign-bond-imf-deal-february",
+ "date": "2026-11-18T06:05:00",
+ "modified": "2026-11-18T06:05:00",
+ "title": "LDC exit set for 2029 as government eyes bonds and a new IMF deal",
+ "excerpt": "The finance minister told Parliament graduation moves to 24 November 2029, and says a first sovereign bond could come within three months.",
+ "categories": [
+  "news",
+  "policy"
+ ],
+ "tags": [
+  "aid-financing",
+  "governance",
+  "poverty-reduction-decent-employment"
+ ],
+ "image": null,
+ "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Bangladesh will now leave the least developed country (LDC) group on 24 November 2029 instead of 24 November 2026, Finance and Planning Minister Amir Khosru Mahmud Chowdhury told Parliament on 16 November. The UN General Assembly approved the three-year extension on 29 October, he said, in reply to a notice from reserved-seat MP Sultana Jasmine (UNB, via The Business Standard).</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">He said graduation will end duty-free market access and the TRIPS flexibilities that support the pharmaceutical industry, which meets 98 percent of domestic demand. A National Roadmap for Smooth, Sustainable and Irreversible LDC Graduation 2026-29 sets out five pillars, 21 action areas and 15 private-sector priorities. A monitoring committee he chairs is meant to meet monthly and has met twice, bdnews24.com reported.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">To make up for shrinking concessional finance, the government is looking at its first sovereign bond and a Bangladesh-focused equity fund in Hong Kong. At a debt conference in Cox's Bazar organised with the Asian Development Bank, Khosru said a bond could be issued in New York or London \"within two to three months\", with Samurai, Panda and local Sukuk bonds to follow, The Daily Star reported. He said a new agreement with the International Monetary Fund is expected by January or February 2027, and that some conditions agreed by the previous, unelected government are hard to carry out quickly.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Key figures</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Tk 1,27,520 crore budgeted for interest payments in 2026-27</li>\n<li>Budget deficit target of 3.6 percent of GDP</li>\n<li>$3.5 million over three years proposed by the commerce ministry under the WTO's Enhanced Integrated Framework, about $2.5 million of it for seven priority areas (bdnews24.com)</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Graduation changes the terms on which donors lend and give to Bangladesh, and the extra three years give NGOs and their funders more time to plan for less grant money. A turn towards commercial bonds means more expensive debt, and the interest bill already takes money that could go to health, education and social protection.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The size and cost of the first sovereign bond, the conditions in a new IMF programme, and whether the roadmap includes measures to protect social spending.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.tbsnews.net/bangladesh/khosru-briefs-parliament-new-pathway-towards-ldc-graduation-2029-1605011\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Business Standard (UNB): Khosru briefs parliament on new pathway towards LDC graduation in 2029, 17 November 2026</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://bdnews24.com/business/3oikrjghzi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">bdnews24.com: LDC graduation roadmap and trade support, 17 November 2026</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.thedailystar.net/business/economy/news/govt-eyes-sovereign-bond-debut-imf-deal-within-3-months-4324801\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Daily Star: Govt eyes sovereign bond debut, IMF deal within 3 months, 16 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/malaysia-bound-workers-medical-tests-begin-approved-centres.json b/src/data/posts/malaysia-bound-workers-medical-tests-begin-approved-centres.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..f11c67e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/data/posts/malaysia-bound-workers-medical-tests-begin-approved-centres.json
@@ -0,0 +1,18 @@
+{
+ "slug": "malaysia-bound-workers-medical-tests-begin-approved-centres",
+ "date": "2026-11-18T06:10:00",
+ "modified": "2026-11-18T06:10:00",
+ "title": "Medical tests start for Malaysia-bound workers at 51 approved centres",
+ "excerpt": "Screening announced for 31 October has begun. Workers pay Tk 8,000 at Malaysia-approved centres, and unapproved centres face closure.",
+ "categories": [
+  "news",
+  "policy"
+ ],
+ "tags": [
+  "migration",
+  "migrant-protection-and-support",
+  "anti-trafficking-and-exploitation-prevention"
+ ],
+ "image": null,
+ "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Full medical screening for workers going to Malaysia has started at centres approved under Malaysia's Foreign Workers Centralised Management System (FWCMS), the Ministry of Expatriates' Welfare said, bdnews24.com reported on 16 November.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The tests had been due to start on 31 October but did not. A ministry official said the reason for the delay was unclear. There are 51 approved centres and the test costs Tk 8,000. Unapproved centres and recruiting agencies are barred from carrying out tests, and those that break the rule face closure or suspension of their overseas recruitment.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A government-managed drive to send 10,000 workers at zero migration cost is under way, with 112 men in the final selection for DHL Supply Chain (Malaysia). Malaysia is also set to list 338 Bangladeshi recruiting agencies. Ariful Haque Choudhury is the Expatriates' Welfare Minister.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Who carries out medical tests, and at what price, decides part of what workers pay to migrate. Tighter control could protect workers, but a Tk 8,000 fee sits uneasily with promises of zero-cost migration. The move follows the government's cap of Tk 1.54 lakh on the cost of going to Malaysia, and migrant-rights NGOs will want to check what workers actually pay.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Whether workers are charged more than the set fee, how the 338 agencies are chosen, and how many of the 10,000 workers actually leave on zero-cost terms.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://bdnews24.com/bangladesh/62kzqxksaa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">bdnews24.com: Medical tests begin for Malaysia-bound workers, 16 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/measles-november-cases-above-october-catch-up-campaign-sylhet-infants.json b/src/data/posts/measles-november-cases-above-october-catch-up-campaign-sylhet-infants.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..578030f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/data/posts/measles-november-cases-above-october-catch-up-campaign-sylhet-infants.json
@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+{
+ "slug": "measles-november-cases-above-october-catch-up-campaign-sylhet-infants",
+ "date": "2026-11-18T06:25:00",
+ "modified": "2026-11-18T06:25:00",
+ "title": "Measles cases rise in November despite catch-up vaccination drive",
+ "excerpt": "Daily cases and admissions in early November are above October's averages. Sylhet reports two more infant deaths, taking its toll to 220.",
+ "categories": [
+  "news",
+  "data"
+ ],
+ "tags": [
+  "health",
+  "health-nutrition",
+  "child-protection",
+  "nutrition"
+ ],
+ "image": null,
+ "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The measles outbreak is not slowing seven weeks after the government stepped up its catch-up vaccination campaign, according to an analysis of Directorate General of Health Services (DGHS) data by The Daily Star. Daily cases and hospital admissions in the first 16 days of November were higher than October's averages.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Key figures</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>November daily average: 1,405 cases and 1,214 admissions, against 1,267 and 1,088 in October (The Daily Star)</li>\n<li>Daily deaths averaged 4.12 in November, slightly down from 4.65 in October</li>\n<li>1,405 admissions in one day, the second-highest since the outbreak began. The highest was 1,415 on 27 July</li>\n<li>249,569 people with symptoms and 25,442 confirmed cases since 15 March (bdnews24.com)</li>\n<li>19.06 lakh more children vaccinated since 27 September. The campaign first targeted about 34 lakh</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In Sylhet, two more infants, aged six months and seven months, died at Jalalabad Ragib Rabeya Medical College Hospital and Osmani Medical College Hospital, bdnews24.com's Bangla edition reported on 17 November. That takes the division's measles death toll to 220, said Mohammad Noore Alam Shamim, assistant director of the Sylhet health department. The division has 1,628 lab-confirmed cases since 1 January.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The DGHS bulletins count only people treated in hospital, so the true number of cases is not known. Reports from the two outlets also give different national death totals, so Devlens is not quoting a single figure until the DGHS publishes a reconciled count.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Babies too young to be fully vaccinated are among those dying, and the catch-up drive has reached just over half of the children it first aimed for. Community health workers, many employed by NGOs, are central to finding missed children and getting sick children to care early. Rising admissions also put pressure on child wards already stretched by dengue.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Whether the vaccination campaign is extended or redirected to districts with the most cases, and whether DGHS publishes clearer death figures.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/measles-outbreak-shows-no-signs-slowing-4325801\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Daily Star: Measles outbreak shows no signs of slowing, 16 November 2026</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://bdnews24.com/health/53qusb18ga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">bdnews24.com: Measles bulletin, 16 November 2026</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://bangla.bdnews24.com/samagrabangladesh/6p0ll5zv09\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">bdnews24.com (Bangla): Two more infants die of measles in Sylhet, 17 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/mongla-coastal-women-fishers-demand-climate-grants-not-loans-cop31.json b/src/data/posts/mongla-coastal-women-fishers-demand-climate-grants-not-loans-cop31.json
