+ ],
+ "image": null,
+ "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Coastal women, fishers and farmers formed a human chain and held a rally on the bank of the Pashur river at Dakshin Kainmari in Mongla, demanding that climate finance be paid as grants rather than loans, The Daily Star reported on 17 November.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Mongla Nagorik Samaj, Khaddo Nirapotta Network (KHANI) and three other organisations organised the event. Speakers also demanded safe drinking water and a just transition, and said the COP31 climate talks had produced no concrete money for salinity-hit coastal communities. They asked for coastal women, fishers and farmers to have a say when the National Adaptation Plan and Bangladesh's climate pledges (NDCs) are drafted.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Speakers said disaster losses between 2015 and 2020 came to Tk 1,79,199 crore, nearly 10 times the losses of the previous six years, and that Bangladesh produces less than 0.5 percent of global emissions.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">\"Climate finance must not become another burden of debt,\" said climate activist Ratna Sheikh. Mohammad Noor Alam Sheikh, president of Mongla Nagorik Samaj, and SM Jane Alam Babu, president of Udayan Jubo Sangha, also spoke.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The demand for grants over loans is Bangladesh's official position at COP31 too, but this rally puts it in the words of the people who live with rising salinity. It is a reminder for NGOs and donors that locally led adaptation means giving coastal communities a real role in planning, not only in delivery.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The final COP31 decisions on adaptation finance when the talks close on 20 November, and whether the government opens the National Adaptation Plan review to coastal community groups.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/dont-burden-climate-victims-debt-4325761\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Daily Star: Don't burden climate victims with debt, 17 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/national-health-hotline-16263-shut-funding-gap-to-return.json b/src/data/posts/national-health-hotline-16263-shut-funding-gap-to-return.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..f1fdad0
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/data/posts/national-health-hotline-16263-shut-funding-gap-to-return.json
@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+{
+ "slug": "national-health-hotline-16263-shut-funding-gap-to-return",
+ "date": "2026-11-18T06:30:00",
+ "modified": "2026-11-18T06:30:00",
+ "title": "Health hotline 16263 went silent over unpaid bills, now set to return",
+ "excerpt": "The free doctor helpline served 11.23 lakh people in six months but stopped on 12 November when its contract lapsed. The ministry says it will be back soon.",
+ "categories": [
+  "news",
+  "policy"
+ ],
+ "tags": [
+  "health",
+  "health-support",
+  "governance",
+  "aid-financing"
+ ],
+ "image": null,
+ "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Shastho Batayan, the government's free health hotline 16263, stopped working at 9pm on 12 November after its contract expired and was not renewed, The Daily Star reported on 17 November. The health ministry has now decided to extend the contract and expects the service to resume \"within a day or two\".</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The line gives free consultations with doctors and guidance on ambulances. It served 11.23 lakh people between 2 May and 31 October and took about 5,000 calls a day before it shut down. It was launched in 2015.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The sector programme that paid for the service ended in June 2024. The operator, Synesis IT, says it went unpaid for about two years. By April, 22 months of bills were unpaid and more than Tk 16 crore was owed, and the company cut its staff by half. The ministry said it struggled to find money for the service under its new project-based budgeting system.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">\"There is no scope for shutting down this public service,\" said ANM Bazlur Rashid, Secretary of the Health Services Division.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Many NGO health workers and community volunteers send people to 16263 for advice, especially at night and in places far from a doctor. The shutdown shows how a basic service can stop when a donor-style programme ends and nothing replaces it in the regular budget. The same risk hangs over other services that were built inside the old health sector programme.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Whether the hotline is back in service this week, whether the unpaid bills are settled, and whether the ministry moves the service into its regular budget rather than short contract extensions.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/national-health-hotline-be-back-service-within-days-4325906\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Daily Star: National health hotline to be back in service within days, 17 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/parliament-undp-mps-orientation-sdg-oversight-public-procurement.json b/src/data/posts/parliament-undp-mps-orientation-sdg-oversight-public-procurement.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..f9f790b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/data/posts/parliament-undp-mps-orientation-sdg-oversight-public-procurement.json
@@ -0,0 +1,18 @@
+{
+ "slug": "parliament-undp-mps-orientation-sdg-oversight-public-procurement",
+ "date": "2026-11-18T05:55:00",
+ "modified": "2026-11-18T05:55:00",
+ "title": "MPs get UNDP training on SDG and spending oversight",
+ "excerpt": "Parliament and UNDP, with Swiss support, opened a two-day course for MPs on tracking SDG progress and public procurement.",
+ "categories": [
+  "news",
+  "events-learning"
+ ],
+ "tags": [
+  "governance",
+  "governance-human-rights",
+  "training-and-workshop"
+ ],
+ "image": null,
+ "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The Parliament Secretariat has started a two-day orientation for members of Parliament on overseeing progress on the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) and the use of public money, UNDP Bangladesh said on 15 November. The course is supported by UNDP and Switzerland under UNDP's Strengthening Institutions, Policies and Services (SIPS) project.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The first day covers SDG progress and tools for parliamentary monitoring. The second covers public procurement rules, transparency and project management.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">\"It is not only the allocation of funds that matters,\" said Speaker Hafiz Uddin Ahmad Bir Bikram. Chief Whip Nurul Islam Moni said: \"Achieving development goals is closely linked to the proper use of public funds.\" Stephen Rodriques, UNDP Resident Representative, told MPs: \"This is not a commitment that Bangladesh made to the United Nations.\"</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Facilitators included Owais Parray, UNDP country economic adviser, Dr Monzur Hossain of the General Economics Division, Prof S M Abdul-Awal, principal coordinator for SDG affairs at the Prime Minister's Office, and Abu Sayed Md. Kamruzzaman, chief executive of the Bangladesh Public Procurement Authority.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Development spending is at a record low and Parliament has just relaxed procurement rules, so the quality of MPs' oversight matters. Accountability NGOs can use committee hearings and MPs' questions as a route to raise gaps in service delivery and spending.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Whether parliamentary standing committees start holding regular hearings on SDG progress and development projects.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.undp.org/bangladesh/press-releases/parliament-and-undp-launch-orientation-strengthen-oversight-sdgs-and-public-resources\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">UNDP Bangladesh: Parliament and UNDP launch orientation to strengthen oversight of SDGs and public resources, 15 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/procurement-law-amended-deprived-bidders-tib-warns-partisan-abuse.json b/src/data/posts/procurement-law-amended-deprived-bidders-tib-warns-partisan-abuse.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..6456b50
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/data/posts/procurement-law-amended-deprived-bidders-tib-warns-partisan-abuse.json
@@ -0,0 +1,18 @@
+{
+ "slug": "procurement-law-amended-deprived-bidders-tib-warns-partisan-abuse",
+ "date": "2026-11-18T06:20:00",
+ "modified": "2026-11-18T06:20:00",
+ "title": "Procurement law eased for 'deprived' bidders; TIB warns of abuse",
+ "excerpt": "Parliament has made experience and turnover rules optional and created a 'deprived persons' category. TIB says it could be used for partisan ends.",
+ "categories": [
+  "news",
+  "policy"
+ ],
+ "tags": [
+  "governance",
+  "governance-human-rights",
+  "aid-financing"
+ ],
+ "image": null,
+ "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Parliament passed the Public Procurement (Second Amendment) Bill, 2026 by voice vote on 16 November, The Business Standard reported. Finance Minister Amir Khosru Mahmud Chowdhury moved the bill, which was first placed before Parliament on 11 November.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">New subsections 25(2) and 25(3) make long experience, high annual turnover and past large contracts optional requirements for bidders. A new Section 36A defines \"deprived persons\" as people who held valid licences or registrations in the past 20 years but could not take part in public procurement. The government can also relax tender rules for domestic industries, new bidders, micro and small enterprises, and women entrepreneurs.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Government procurement is worth roughly $30 billion a year. It makes up 45 percent of the national budget and 85 percent of Annual Development Programme spending, according to the Bangladesh Public Procurement Authority's 2024-25 annual report, the paper said.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What experts said</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Iftekharuzzaman, executive director of Transparency International Bangladesh (TIB): \"The unprecedented introduction of the 'deprived' category could become a convenient tool for partisan abuse of power.\"</li>\n<li>Zahid Hussain, former lead economist at the World Bank's Dhaka office: \"Those previously excluded over irregularities can now participate and compete normally.\"</li>\n<li>Faruk Hossain, former director general of the Central Procurement Technical Unit: \"Awarding contracts to specific individuals cannot be the objective of a law.\"</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Public contracts pay for the schools, clinics, roads and shelters that NGOs work alongside, so who wins them affects quality on the ground. Looser rules could open the door to small firms and women-led businesses, but TIB's warning points to a risk of contracts going to the politically connected.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">How the government defines and applies the \"deprived\" category in practice, and whether the procurement authority publishes data on who wins contracts under the new rules.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://www.tbsnews.net/bangladesh/law-order/govt-eases-tender-rules-deprived-bidders-under-amended-procurement-law-1604876\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Business Standard: Govt eases tender rules for 'deprived' bidders under amended procurement law, 17 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
+}
diff --git a/src/data/posts/public-toilet-policy-2026-safe-water-2030-target-rangpur.json b/src/data/posts/public-toilet-policy-2026-safe-water-2030-target-rangpur.json
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..167f9fb
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/data/posts/public-toilet-policy-2026-safe-water-2030-target-rangpur.json
@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+{
+ "slug": "public-toilet-policy-2026-safe-water-2030-target-rangpur",
+ "date": "2026-11-18T06:00:00",
+ "modified": "2026-11-18T06:00:00",
+ "title": "Government targets safe water for all by 2030 under new WASH plans",
+ "excerpt": "Officials in Rangpur set out the National Public Toilet Policy 2026 and a WASH plan to 2040, aiming for 80% access to safe public toilets by 2030.",
+ "categories": [
+  "news",
+  "policy"
+ ],
+ "tags": [
+  "wash",
+  "sanitation",
+  "water-resource",
+  "hygiene-promotion"
+ ],
+ "image": null,
+ "content": "<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The government aims to provide safe water to everyone and safe public toilets to 80 percent of people by 2030, Rangpur Divisional Commissioner Md Jahangir Alam said at a workshop in Rangpur on 17 November, BSS reported in The Financial Express.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The workshop promoted the National Public Toilet Policy 2026 and the WASH sector development plan for 2026 to 2040. The policy requires public toilets in transit areas, institutions, crowded public places, low-income communities, event venues and emergencies.</p>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Thirty-eight government departments and 10 NGOs took part. Md Abu Jafar, Rangpur divisional director of the Local Government Division (LGD), presided. Suyemen Joe, deputy secretary in the LGD's policy support wing, and Rangpur Deputy Commissioner Israt Jahan also spoke.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why it matters</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">WASH NGOs build and run much of the water and sanitation in poor and remote areas, so a national policy and a plan to 2040 give them a framework to line up with and a basis to hold the government to its targets. Public toilets for low-income communities and emergencies are areas where NGOs already work, from urban slums to cyclone shelters.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What to watch</h3>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">How the toilet policy is funded and who maintains the facilities, and whether the WASH plan sets yearly targets that NGOs and the public can track.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Sources</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a href=\"https://thefinancialexpress.com.bd/national/govt-working-to-ensure-100pc-safe-water-supply-by-2030\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Financial Express (BSS): Govt working to ensure 100pc safe water supply by 2030, 17 November 2026</a></li>\n</ul>"
+}
